<a href="#allar">Drew Allar</a>
<a href="#klubnik">Cade Klubnik</a>
<a href="#green">The Class's Real Testing Standout</a>
<a href="#pattern">Why Top QBs Skip the Drills</a>
<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="mendoza">Fernando Mendoza, Raiders</h2>
<p>The Heisman Trophy winner and No. 1 overall pick measured 6-foot-5 and 225 pounds at the combine — prototypical size for the position — but chose not to throw or run any drills in Indianapolis, saving his workout for private sessions instead. Mendoza's game was never built on measurable speed; scouts consistently pointed to his post-snap processing and quick release as his defining traits, with his mobility described as "good-but-not-great" rather than a primary weapon. That profile puts him closer to a traditional pocket passer than a dual-threat, which is part of why teams were comfortable evaluating him almost entirely off tape rather than a stopwatch.</p>
<h2 id="allar">Drew Allar, Vikings</h2>
<p>Just four months removed from ankle surgery, Allar showed up to the combine but only participated in throwing drills with receivers, skipping the 40-yard dash and other testing. Per NFL Network's Daniel Jeremiah, Allar measured about a half-inch taller than Mendoza with a wider wingspan, longer arms, and larger hands — measurables that check every box teams look for at the position, even without a timed number attached to them.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
