<ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
<li><strong>Fernando Mendoza (Raiders, No. 1 overall):</strong> 6'5", 225 lbs — no drills run</li>
<li><strong>Drew Allar (Vikings):</strong> Throwing only — no 40, coming off ankle surgery</li>
<li><strong>Cade Klubnik (Jets, Round 4):</strong> 4.65s 40 (pro day), 33.5" vertical — full testing</li>
</ul>
</div>
<h2 id="klubnik">Cade Klubnik, Jets</h2>
<p>Unlike Mendoza and Allar, Klubnik chose to test — but did it at his Clemson pro day rather than the combine itself, running a 4.65-second 40-yard dash and posting a 33.5-inch vertical jump at 207 pounds. Klubnik said he wanted to "go be an athlete" after a season limited by an ankle injury that capped his mobility, and the numbers back up a player whose calling card includes functional athleticism to extend plays — solid but not spectacular testing for the position, which tracks with his fall to the fourth round after a difficult final college season.</p>
