<h2 id="pattern">Why Top QBs Skip the Drills</h2>
<p>It's a familiar pattern at the position: the more a quarterback's evaluation rests on processing, accuracy, and pocket presence rather than pure athleticism, the less incentive there is to risk an injury or a bad number in a testing environment that doesn't reflect game conditions. Kyler Murray famously skipped every drill at his own 2019 combine and went No. 1 overall anyway — <a class="inline" href="https://athletecalculator.com/kyler-murray-vikings-starting-qb-athletic-profile">read our full breakdown of that decision and what his rushing production revealed instead</a>. Mendoza and Allar are following the same logic: when tape and interviews already answer the questions teams have, a 40 time is a risk with little reward.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Why didn't Fernando Mendoza run the 40-yard dash at the combine?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Mendoza chose not to participate in any on-field testing drills at the 2026 combine, focusing instead on interviews and medical evaluations. As the presumptive No. 1 overall pick, his tape and processing ability were already well established, making testing an unnecessary risk.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Is it common for top quarterback prospects to skip combine drills?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Yes, particularly for pocket passers whose evaluation is driven by accuracy and decision-making rather than athleticism. Kyler Murray, Josh Rosen, and other top picks have skipped some or all combine drills in past years without it affecting their draft stock significantly.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Who tested the best among 2026's rookie quarterbacks?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Arkansas's Taylen Green set combine records for the position with a 4.36-second 40-yard dash, a 43.5-inch vertical jump, and an 11-foot-2 broad jump — all-time bests for a quarterback at the event.</div>
</div>
